\paragraph{Attention reorganization at L1.}
We analyze per-head attention distributions at L1 for the readout token position (Figure~\ref{fig:attention}).
In the trained model, 25 out of 32 attention heads are instruction-dominant: they allocate on average $58\%$ of attention weight to instruction tokens (${\sim}8\%$ of the sequence) and only $20.9\%$ to image tokens.
In the untrained model, 0 out of 32 heads are instruction-dominant: attention defaults to image tokens ($87.8\%$) with only $11.7\%$ allocated to instruction tokens.

\paragraph{Deliberate vs.\ incidental routing.}
Both models achieve near-perfect instruction classification at L1 because instruction embeddings from the frozen tokenizer are inherently highly distinguishable: even $12\%$ incidental attention provides enough signal for a linear probe.
The critical difference is robustness.
Trained deliberate routing (58\% attention, 25/32 dedicated heads) creates a signal strong enough to survive 30 subsequent layers ($100\%$ at L31).
Untrained incidental routing ($12\%$ attention, 0/32 dedicated heads) produces a fragile signal that degrades to $82\%$ by L31.
These results suggest that training does not create routing from scratch (the architecture provides incidental leakage) but strengthens it from fragile to robust through a different attention strategy.

\paragraph{Asymmetric training effects.}
This routing mechanism is consistent with why training effects concentrate at the readout token rather than image tokens: training reshapes how the readout token attends to instruction tokens (attention $+46$pp), maintains routing through depth ($+18$pp at L31), and improves functional decodability ($R^2$ $+0.069$), while image\_mean metrics change minimally ($\Delta \leq 1.7\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:training_effect_summary}).
Note that this comparison uses a randomly initialized LLM baseline rather than the pretrained Llama-2 checkpoint; the observed routing changes may therefore reflect a combination of language pretraining and VLA fine-tuning (see \S\ref{sec:discussion} for discussion).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig8_attention.pdf}
\caption{L1 attention from the readout token, per head (sorted by instruction attention fraction). \textbf{(a)}~Trained: 25/32 heads are instruction-dominant ($>50\%$ attention to instruction tokens, blue). \textbf{(b)}~Untrained: 0/32 heads are instruction-dominant; nearly all attention goes to image tokens (orange). Training learns deliberate instruction routing, creating a robust signal maintained through L31 ($100\%$) vs.\ untrained decay ($82\%$).}
\label{fig:attention}
\end{figure}

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Three-level routing gradient at L1.}
To disentangle the contributions of language pretraining and VLA fine-tuning, we compare L1 attention distributions at the readout token across three conditions: untrained (random initialization), pretrained Llama-2 base (no VLA fine-tuning), and VLA-trained (Table~\ref{tab:routing_gradient}; Figure~\ref{fig:attention}).
The three conditions form a monotonic gradient in instruction routing.
The untrained model allocates 0/32 instruction-dominant heads ($11.7\%$ text attention, $87.8\%$ image).
Llama-2 base shows partial routing: 9/32 heads become instruction-dominant ($36.5\%$ text, $61.7\%$ image), indicating that language pretraining seeds instruction-attending structure.
VLA fine-tuning amplifies this to 25/32 instruction-dominant heads ($57.7\%$ text, $21.3\%$ image), a $9 \to 25$ head reorganization that demonstrates active restructuring beyond inherited language priors.

\paragraph{Routing robustness across depth.}
The three-level gradient extends to cross-instruction classification robustness.
The VLA-trained model achieves $100\%$ 10-way accuracy at L1 and maintains it through L31.
The untrained model also achieves $100\%$ at L1 (instruction embeddings from the frozen tokenizer are inherently distinguishable) but decays to $82\%$ by L31, reflecting fragile incidental routing.
Llama-2 base shows an intermediate pattern: L1 accuracy is $80.2\%$ (below both other conditions), rising to $100\%$ by L5.
The lower L1 accuracy suggests that pretrained representations encode general language structure rather than instruction identity per se, requiring additional layers to achieve full discrimination.

\paragraph{Asymmetric training effects.}
This routing gradient is consistent with why training effects concentrate at the readout token rather than image tokens: VLA fine-tuning reshapes how the readout token attends to instruction tokens (attention $+21$pp over Llama-2 base, $+46$pp over untrained), while image\_mean metrics change minimally ($\Delta \leq 1.7\%$; Appendix~\ref{sec:training_effect_summary}).

