\paragraph{Representation similarity and probing methodology.}
RSA~\cite{kriegeskorte2008}, SVCCA~\cite{raghu2017}, projection-weighted CCA~\cite{morcos2018}, and CKA~\cite{kornblith2019} compare geometric structure between representation spaces; linear probes~\cite{alain2017,hewitt2019} and the broader probing literature~\cite{belinkov2022} test whether specific information is decodable from intermediate representations.
We adopt RSA as our primary geometric metric and MLP probes as our functional metric, with CKA in the appendix, distinguishing representational structure from information accessibility.

tool_args.old_string:
\paragraph{Token specialization and routing.}
The strict division of labor between image and readout tokens suggests that the LLM does not blend modalities uniformly across token types.
The three-condition comparison (0/32 $\to$ 9/32 $\to$ 25/32 instruction-dominant heads) shows that routing develops incrementally: language pretraining seeds partial instruction-attending structure, and VLA fine-tuning actively reorganizes attention beyond this prior, with effects concentrated at the readout token ($R^2$ $+0.069$) rather than image representations ($\Delta \leq 1.7\%$).

